{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filename": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filepath": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "page": 1, "title": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "year": "2026"}, "page_content": "Emollients and moisturisers:\u00a0Emollients are substances that soften and soothe the skin, while moisturisers primarily work to hydrate the skin by trapping water in the outermost layer.\n\nHow to Use Topical Treatments Effectively\n\nConsistency is Key:\u00a0Regular application as prescribed is crucial for managing symptoms effectively.\n\nFollow Directions:\u00a0Each treatment may have specific instructions regarding application, dosage, and duration of use. Always follow the advice of your healthcare provider.\n\nCombination Therapy:\u00a0Sometimes, combining different topical treatments can enhance effectiveness. For example, using a corticosteroid in the morning and a vitamin D analogue in the evening.\n\nRotational Therapy:\u00a0To avoid side effects, your doctor may recommend rotating between different topical treatments.\n\nPotential Side Effects\n\nWhile topical treatments are generally safe, they can cause side effects, especially with prolonged use. Common side effects include:\n\n\n\nSkin Irritation:\u00a0Redness, itching, or burning sensation.\n\nThinning of the Skin:\u00a0Particularly with prolonged use of potent corticosteroids.\n\nStretch Marks:\u00a0Can occur with long-term use of topical steroids.", "type": "Document"}}